 {:seon.task/name "evaluations"
  :seon.task/plan "The model's context is a sequence of real evaluations, rendered from their records by the one printer's :ai face; no hand-built sections (owner, 2026-10-23: \"stick with a system message that we put in via (help) and then a pull and walk render in simple clojure code so there's no magic and we output data for the responses for the agent and it should be cache stable and grow with transcript size mostly except the dynamic portions which should be at the end\"; \"everything is structured as separate evals where we show the forms the agent input (or we generate the help and agents entity walk) and they can clearly see how results come back with timing and individual renders being called so they can see what functions are rendering what data and where the data is so they can update it to update their context generation intuitively\"; \"everything is real and is structured just like the model ran them and recorded and rendered with render functions not manual wiring\"; \"These need to be cachable so we aren't literatlly re-running things\"; \"now can be a named function\"). Root's ruling (decisions.md, 2026-10-23): no live agent runs until the context has a format agents respond well to; each version is scored by agent-loop's judge (about 10 real task contexts, the first move scored blind) and read by Haiku readers (notes/context-reader.md, from one request: {:op :context :task}); keep each change small enough to score often. What exists: seon.context/render builds the system message and the current-state block from hand-built sections (seon.context/current-state, sections, request-text), seon.turn/basis! records a turn's basis, and the context is remade from it. Build, in order, each step scored: (1) the system message is (help)'s text (my.agent/help), stable: markdown, a doc like any wiki page, whose citations seon.ref/parse reads and the context pulls as it pulls any page's (owner, 2026-10-23: \"If help is best a markdown style response and then we parse it for refs that works better I think\"); it says plainly what the views are made of: the walk, the one printer, each value's declared render, and the page's Tailwind (classes in hiccup, regenerated by bin/build), and it cites the docs an agent needs (docs/render.md, docs/references.md for the citation format, the Tailwind setup) at the depth each should be pulled, so they reach every context through the citation mechanism (owner, 2026-10-23: \"Make sure the help text is clear that we are using tailwind and put any refs in there that need ot be pulled in to documentation\"); (2) turn 0 is evaluations the turn loop makes and records as the model's are (origin :context): (help)'s sections it needs, then (my.agent/walk) of the agent's own entity (seon.render/walk: agent, run, task, plan, the plan's citations at their depth, the proofs and how each proof's db is made, from its namespace's fixtures: agent-loop's step E run 11, the model never found them); (3) each later turn appends the model's calls: each evaluation's ;; thought, form, result by the result's own :ai render, and its cost as recorded when it ran (ticks, ms and bytes: seon.turn/a-turn-shows-its-cost-against-its-budget), rendered from that fact, which never changes after its turn, so it is byte-identical every turn and the cache holds (owner's ruling, decisions.md 2026-10-23: cost numbers belong in the context, so models learn how expensive things are and understand warnings and limits); what moves is only in the last evaluation, (now), after the cached prefix, and (now) holds only what changes the agent's next move (owner, 2026-10-23: \"only put really useful shit in (now) and no adding up each eval time isn't useful\"): no sums of the evaluations' costs, no stored totals; a reused result reads exactly like a fresh one (decisions.md 2026-10-23); a recorded evaluation is rendered from its record and never run again, so every earlier turn renders the same bytes and the provider's cache holds (a cost proof: the prefix is byte-identical across turns, and the context grows with the transcript; proofs: an evaluation's cost text is byte-identical at turn n and turn n+1, and what moves (proofs now, turns left) appears only in (now), after the prefix); (3b) byte-stable, verified by the provider (owner, 2026-10-23: \"I want byte stable context gen that we verify is being cached properly by the models\"): a proof renders a run's context at turn n and n+1 and asserts turn n's text is a byte-identical prefix of turn n+1's but for the last evaluation (seon.context-test/the-prefix-stays-byte-identical-across-turns, kept for the new format); each model turn records the tokens of the prefix it shares with the turn before beside the provider's cache-hit tokens (:seon.turn/usage), and a turn whose hit falls short of that prefix by more than the provider's cache block (DeepSeek: 64 tokens) is an open problem fact naming the turn and the gap, never silence; the run's page shows each turn's hit share; one short paid run of a few turns on one task verifies it against the real provider once the format scores well (task a-stable-prefix-is-sent-once); (4) the last evaluation, each turn, is (my.agent/now), the owner's six (2026-10-23, all approved), each present only when it has something to say: am I done (the proofs red now, each failing assertion's expected and actual, what changed since the last turn); what did I break (problems its own change caused: a proof outside its task now red, a contract gap, a stale wiki section; the merge refuses on these); did the world move (main moved, so merge main; another agent changed a var it changed; a task it needs merged); did someone tell me something (new messages); how much room is left (turns left, a warning only near a limit, no tally); what have I already read (so nothing is read twice). Its value is the full data; its :ai render is compact and beautiful, one line a fact, and the agent digs into the value (its handle) for what the render left out (owner: \"I want this compact and beuriful and the agent can dig into the result if they want more detail that maybe the render hid\"). Its pieces: proofs now, what this run has read already (task the-state-names-what-was-read), the one part that breaks the cache; (5) the citations a string holds are shown as citations with what they resolved to, so an agent learns that writing seon.ref's format in any string field brings the cited data in; (6) which render made which part shows as the measured readers find useful, not more (owner: \"mostly helpful data not just a shitload of data\"). Deletes seon.context's section builders (current-state, sections, the request assembly) as each step replaces them; agent-loop's found and ns-map go when the walk's citations replace them (agent-loop: nothing of theirs depends on them)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render/walk"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/turn-zero-is-recorded-evaluations seon.context-test/a-recorded-evaluation-renders-the-same-bytes seon.context-test/now-is-the-last-evaluation seon.context-cost-test/the-context-grows-with-the-transcript seon.turn-test/a-turn-whose-cache-misses-its-prefix-is-a-problem seon.context-test/an-evaluations-cost-text-is-the-same-every-turn seon.context-test/what-moves-is-only-in-now}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-request-is-a-conversation-of-evals"
  :seon.task/plan "Step 1 of notes/design-agent-view-2026-10-24.md (approved, docs/decisions.md 2026-10-24). The request is a render of the agent's record (owner, 2026-10-24): each record's :seon.render/ai renders its part as provider-neutral messages (`seon.context/eval-messages`, `seon.context/message-messages`), `seon.context/request` composes them in tx order, and one provider format function, `seon.model/wire-messages`, makes the wire shape; :seon.config.ai/replay-reasoning says whether reasoning goes back. A function-calling conversation: per model turn its prose (the reply's content, stored on its first call's eval), its reasoning replayed exactly as returned, its eval calls, and each call's response as a tool message read from its eval record; the messages a turn took sit before its reply; a form the user evaluated in the agent is a user message carrying its response; the screen ends it. One response per call (`seon.repl/response`, :seon.eval/response): {:handle :value :out :err :ex :ms :ticks :bytes}, each key only when present, the forms run in order and stopped by the first exception, printed by the one printer under its handle result/e<n> (`seon.repl/printed`), which holds the response; n counts the branch's evals, so a handle is unique across restarts. One eval record per call (:seon.eval/*, was :seon.cluster.eval/*; :seon.eval/by, was :origin). tool_choice auto: a prose-only reply ends the turn and the run waits for the user. No ;; reaches the model (the eval tool, docs/agent.md). Deletes: `seon.repl/wrap-print`'s render path, the per-form items and handles, `seon.context/fold-evals`, `shown-eval`, `shown-evals`, `transcript`, the run/turn message split's 'Nothing ran' message, `seon.render.ai/items`, the eval tool's thinking argument, :lead on evals."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-request-is-a-conversation-of-evals-and-the-page
                      seon.repl-test/a-call-is-one-response}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-turn-records-what-it-sent"
  :seon.task/plan "Step 3 of notes/design-agent-view-2026-10-24.md section 10 (revision 3): record what each turn sent, once at send time, and /agent/{id}/debug as the :seon.render/html view of those records, drawn by root's render proc from its cache (step 2), with no request builder and no provider format on that path (owner via root, 2026-10-24). Three parts, each reported with its numbers.
(A) The record, at send time (the owner's ruling 2026-10-24: :seon.turn/sent only, no text entities). seon.context/request answers, beside its messages, the records they render, in order: [:section eid t] the help page's own section as of t, [:task eid t] the task as of its run's first turn, [:eval id], [:message id], [:history n] (n earlier records the history budget left out; its message is a function of n). seon.turn/act! writes the turn with its basis and these refs as the request goes, committed, and its reply and usage, or its typed error (:seon.turn/error), when the call ends: one turn entity either way. seon.context/sent-parts reads a turn back, each record by its own render. Deletes the remake path (remade, run-text, recorded). Proofs: seon.context-test/a-turn-records-what-it-sent (read back equals sent, after the task and the help page changed), a-turn-records-one-turns-messages (no text in the record); seon.agent-test/a-failed-call-keeps-what-it-sent.
(B) The debug page. /agent/{id}/debug is a view of the agent entity (web view-of `<id>/debug`, as /agent/{id} is), whose page lists the agent's model turns newest first; each turn's row is its :seon.turn/sent refs, each resolved to its record and shown by that record's :seon.render/ai messages through one :seon.render/html render of a sent request (role, content, reasoning folded, each eval call's prose and code, a tool message under its call, the screen evals set apart, tokens and cache hits from the turn's usage, and beside each part the eval, message or text that made it: the owner's 2026-10-24 ruling, \"styled ... show the form and eval result that created it\"). Drawn by the render proc like any page: its walk is recorded (step 2 B'), a new turn touches it (step 2 C), and only the new turn's row renders (units by input). Reuses: render.proc's page path, ui/page's units, the request render's styling from the set-aside jolt/agent-conversation-debug-wip (its hiccup, not its request rebuild). ~70 lines. Proofs: seon.render-test/the-debug-page-shows-what-each-turn-sent (the page's messages for a turn equal the wire body's, as data), seon.render-test/a-new-turn-is-one-new-row (rendered units: one); checked in a browser on this worktree's root with a scripted run.
(C) Cost proof for the page: one new turn costs one turn's messages (renders: the new row only; the walk is the page's, O(turns) until step 4 makes rows their own components, stated in the proof).
Not in this step: per-entity components and the screen as a read of root's page (step 4); recording the provider's own wire bytes (the record is provider-neutral; the wire body is a pure function of it)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.render/a-view-renders-once-per-change-of-its-reads"}
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-turn-records-what-it-sent
                      seon.context-test/a-turn-records-one-turns-messages
                      seon.render-test/the-debug-page-shows-what-each-turn-sent
                      seon.render-test/a-new-turn-is-one-new-row
                      seon.render-cost-test/a-new-turn-costs-one-turns-messages}}
 {:seon.task/name "history-steps-hold-below-one-step"
  :seon.task/plan "From the branch review of jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-25, root's scratchpad branch-review.md, follow-ups): `seon.context/kept` raises each boundary to a multiple of :seon.config.render/history-step but clamps it to the newest record, so while a run has no more records than one step and its history is over the budget, the boundary moves every turn and the prefix the provider caches breaks each turn. Correct: below one step the boundary holds as above it. Proof: a kept case of fewer parts than the step, over the budget, whose picks hold for two growing histories. Done otherwise (2026-10-25, worktree the-history-cut-holds-between-jumps): the step is gone. A resumed run (a-map #2, 35-39k tokens a request, past the 32k budget) recorded requests that shared only their first two parts (962 tokens) on 22 of 28 turns: the two boundaries, recomputed each turn, moved back and forth (e23, e39, e7, e55 ...), and the provider served 23% of 1.1M tokens from its cache. `seon.context/kept` now walks the parts oldest first and holds its two cuts, advancing them only when what is sent passes the budget, to half of it, so between jumps each request is the one before and what was added; the left-out note moved from last to just after the task's data (sent last, it broke every prefix by one message). Re-rendered offline from that run's records (notes/measure/history-cut-rerender.clj): the share of each request shared with the one before 22.5% -> 93.7%, 23 breaks -> 1 in 29 turns, tokens a turn 35.1k -> 27.3k. :seon.config.render/history-step deleted."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "printed-parts-are-keyed-by-their-ref"
  :seon.task/plan "From the branch review of jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-25, root's scratchpad branch-review.md, follow-ups): `seon.context/printed-run` keys :parts by the record's bare id string, shared by evals (e5) and messages (m-...); an eval and a message of one id would take one slot. Correct: key by the ref ([:eval id], [:message id]). Proof: a run holding an eval and a message of the same id prints both."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-debug-read-back-prints-with-its-turns-config"
  :seon.task/plan "From the branch review of jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-25, root's scratchpad branch-review.md, follow-ups): `seon.context/parts-of` prints a turn's records with the config in force now (budgets through cfg/value), so it equals what the turn sent only while the config is unchanged. Correct: the read-back prints with the config as of the turn (seon.config/from-db as of the turn's commit). Proof: a turn read back after a budget changes equals what it sent."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "a-request-costs-its-new-records"
  :seon.task/plan "From the branch review of jolt/agent-conversation (2026-10-25, root's scratchpad branch-review.md, follow-ups): `seon.context/records` scans every eval of the agent per turn (a >= filter after the index scan), and `request` does O(history) work in kept and in building what it sends; the cost proof (seon.context-cost-test) covers printed-run only. Correct: the request reads records from the last printed one by index, and kept's picks are kept with the cache, so a turn costs its new records; or the request is in the cost proof with what it costs per record held. Proof: seon.context-cost-test with `request` flat in the records held."
  :seon.task/kind :work}
 {:seon.task/name "the-screen-shows-the-sections-nearest-the-plan"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-25: embed the docs and pull in related concepts that we can refine over the session. What exists: every wiki section has a vector on main (docs/vector.md#what-is-embedded, `{:of :seon.section/address}` restricts ```seon.vector/near``` to sections), the plan has a vector, and the task's data shows only the sections the plan cites (```seon.context/task-data``` :sections). A one-line request cites nothing, so its agent starts with no section and no write set (dogfood, notes/dogfood-and-docs-2026-10-25.md gap 3: about ten evals each of hand exploration). The change: the turn's part (the screen, made again each turn, so it follows the plan as the agent rewrites it; the run's part stays fixed for the prompt cache) shows :related, the k sections nearest the plan's current vector that the plan does not cite, each at summary depth through ```seon.docs/view``` (address and first sentence), with the form that reads one whole (`(my.agent/help \"docs/page.md#anchor\")` once `seon.docs/a-section-reads-at-each-level` merges, else `seon.docs/view`). k is a config key read through the one resolver like `:seon.config.render/similar` (a new key: name it, default, unit and what 0 means, and say why `:seon.config.render/similar` cannot serve; that is the owner's gate, approved in principle by the request above). Computed only when the plan's vector changed (the render cache keyed by its reads: an unchanged plan costs nothing). Measure on the five fit requests (`seon.fixtures/fit-requests`, scripted provider, no paid calls): which related sections a scripted agent reads, and tokens per turn; then a paid comparison waits for root's go. Decided (2026-10-25, this worktree): the key is :seon.config.render/related, unit sections, default 5, 0 shows none; :seon.config.render/similar cannot serve: it counts program functions, in the task's data (the run's fixed part, for the prompt cache), and is off by step 6's measurement, while these are sections on the screen that follow the plan. ```seon.context/related``` ranks the sections and ```seon.context/related-at``` reuses its answer while the count and the branch's last vector write are the same. The owner, 2026-10-25, on the first version (an entry of my.task/status): \"I want everything to be explicit evals in our dynamic eval so the embedding references can be dynamic and refined as the session goes on\", and enough shown that agents dig for more: so it is ```my.task/related```, a form of ```seon.screen/forms``` (recorded as an eval each turn), and ```my.agent/related``` for the agent's own text or a var and a count (the screen form asks no effect, so no test reaching the screen reaches a process), each line at summary depth with the forms that read one whole and search further (```seon.context/related-view```); the read form is seon.docs/view until my.agent/help reads any address."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-related-part-shows-the-sections-nearest-the-plan seon.context-test/related-sections-follow-a-rewritten-plan seon.context-cost-test/related-sections-cost-nothing-when-the-plan-is-unchanged}
  :seon.task/needs #{"seon.vector/a-text-written-on-a-branch-is-embedded-there"}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-task-pulls-what-agents-read"
  :seon.task/plan "Kept, not merged (root, 2026-10-25): the pulls are built on their own branch, behind :seon.config.render/pulls (off by default), and come back when the pipeline pilot can measure turns to green with them on against off (AGENTS.md section 3: a key nothing measured has asked for waits). What they pull into the task's data (seon.context/task-data), from what agents read by eval that the task's data could have shown (measured 2026-10-25 on the 8 runs whose eval forms survive, 143 reads: 12% already shown, about a third pullable): :test-ns, each proof namespace's ns form and helpers a line each (the proof's test namespace was read in 7 of 8 runs); :functions, the my.* functions the system prompt names in its code spans (15 source reads of my.task/status); :seeded n, a design plan citing no var gets the n functions nearest it (dogfood: about ten evals of hand exploration each); :call-sites, each caller's call (read in its namespace, the head resolved by ns-resolve, only where :form/refs name the var); :callees, each write-set var's program callees a line each; :example-test, one test calling it that is not a proof. Measured cost on the branch (estimated tokens, chars/4 of the uncut task data, before the printer's budget), on a task citing 8 vars (task data 5,821) and one citing 1 (1,778): :test-ns +1,079 / +1,587, :functions +191, :call-sites +971 / +30, :callees +2,508 / +479 (ten times its 240 estimate on a wide write set: cut to the k nearest, or a count), :example-test +805 / +182; :seeded proven, not measured on a real plan. Its self-review rated it overbuilt as long as nothing measures it. The pilot measures: the five fit requests (seon.fixtures/fit-requests), each pull on against all off, turns to green and tokens per turn; keep a pull only where turns fall."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-task-pulls-what-its-config-names}}
 {:seon.task/name "related-weighs-a-neighbour-by-its-rarity"
  :seon.task/plan "```seon.context/related``` ranks the call graph's functions by how many of the plan's cited vars reach each (by-frequency over their callers and callees), so what every contracted form names comes first: on a worktree root (2026-10-25, the browser check of seon.context/the-screen-shows-the-sections-nearest-the-plan) its five functions were seon.contract/checked, seon.contract/on?, seon.agent/db, seon.contract/error? and seon.db/eid, the plumbing each `defn` expands to or calls, none of what the task changes. The comparison that set the default (docs/vector.md#what-the-work-is-likely-to-need-as-an-eval: functions 0.50 at 5 fused) counted the same callees, so this is the measured default's noise, not a regression. The field's way: weigh a neighbour by its inverse document frequency (log of the forms over the forms naming it: one AVET count of `:form/refs` a candidate), so a callee every form names weighs nothing. Measure first: rerun notes/related-search/search.clj's graph ranking with IDF weights on the same 32 merged tasks; keep it only if functions R@5 rises. No new attribute: the counts are seeks on the index there is. Measured 2026-10-25 (worktree related-weighs-a-neighbour-by-its-rarity, on its root with every form, section, schema and plan embedded; notes/related-search/search3.clj and search3b.clj over search.clj, results in results3.edn and results3b.edn): the 24 merged tasks whose merge changed functions their plan does not cite, what the plan cites left out as related does. The graph alone at 5: 0.099 by count, 0.214 with IDF (8 tasks better, none worse). Fused with meaning, what the screen shows: R@5 0.343 to 0.353 (3 better, 1 worse, 20 the same), R@10 0.401 to 0.423. Of the fused top 5 over 31 tasks (155 lines), plumbing (seon.contract/checked, on?, error?, seon.config/value, seon.agent/db, seon.db/eid) filled 26 by count and 1 with IDF: the lines change, the recall barely does, because what replaces them is rarely what the merge changed either. Built (root, 2026-10-25: \"don't render crap\": 26 of 155 screen lines were plumbing): a neighbour weighs 1/(1 + the forms naming it), counted by bounded-count to :seon.config.render/related-rarity (16), which needs no count of every form (log IDF would) and reads at most 16 datoms a neighbour, so the graph half costs O(neighbours x 16) more. Measured at caps 16, 64 and 256 alike (notes/related-search/search3c.clj): fused R@5 0.346 against 0.343 by counts (log IDF 0.353), plumbing lines 26 to 0 of 155. On two live tasks' screens (seon.context/a-first-turns-context-costs-what-it-shows, seon.problems/a-problem-row-holds-what-it-knows) seon.contract/checked and on? gave way to seon.ref/kept, seon.task/citation-problems, seon.ref/cited and seon.render.ai/problems, seon.check/health-check: the lines changed, not their number. The :macro clause stays (a macro reached by meaning is weighed by nothing)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/related-fuses-the-plans-references-with-meaning}}
 {:seon.task/name "the-request-has-one-path"
  :seon.task/plan "Found building revision 4.2 (jolt/model-reads-the-page): seon.context/request keeps a branch for a request with no agent (a test's seon.context/messages), which prints the block's task sections (task-message) and turn sections (state-message) itself, beside the page every agent request reads. Production never takes it (every model turn and outside read names its agent), so it is a second path kept for tests: move the tests that call seon.context/messages (context-test's each-thing-is-said-once, each-section-is-sent-once-per-request, the-turn-names-what-the-merge-will-carry and their kin) onto an agent's request, asserting what the page's [:task] and [:status] parts hold, then delete messages, the no-agent branch, task-message, state-message and task-keys. Proof: no caller of seon.context/messages is left (a resolve of it is nil), and the moved tests pass on the page."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/the-request-is-a-conversation-of-evals-and-the-page}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-prose-request-finds-its-functions"
  :seon.task/plan "A user's request in prose cites no var, so the page's functions came from meaning alone (```seon.context/related```: its graph half empty), and near a request the forms nearest are mostly its tests: the design-build run db1's pages cited seon.print/seen for the mixed collection (the site: seon.print.select/fill-rows) and seon.ref/shown for the summary (the site: seon.context/related-view). v1 showed no functions for a prose request (its must-reads were the plan's citations). A plan citing no defined var seeds the graph half with the first program functions among the :seon.config.render/related-seeds-near forms nearest it (its callers and callees by rarity, fused by rrf as before); the meaning candidates' count is :seon.config.render/related-near (the literal 4 it was). Measured on the 8 db1 requests, each true site in the page's functions (cited: transport-error and my.agent/help, which their plans name; or related's five): before 4 of 8 (long-name, short-history in related); seeded from the 20 candidates 5 (+ summary); from the 50 nearest forms 6 (+ mixed: fill-rows); from 200, 6. A quoted-text grep (seon.docs/grep of the plan's code-like fragments) found only what meaning found; the task's namespace as a prior helped one and missed another. The two misses (a-proof-that-never-ran-says-so, a-map-shows-its-first-entry) describe behaviour main had already fixed: reproducing first (the one-go instructions) finds that, not the page. A plan citing a defined var keeps today's graph (docs/vector.md#fused-with-the-call-graph's 31-task measurement holds)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-test/a-prose-requests-functions-come-from-its-nearest-and-their-neighbours seon.context-test/related-fuses-the-plans-references-with-meaning}}]
